  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 404 AM MST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nMidlevel flow becomes more southwesterly today ahead of an advancing \ntrough along the West Coast. This will lead to another warm and dry \nday, even warmer than yesterday for most with highs peaking near 10 \ndegrees above normal. The Wind Corridor from eastern Sweetwater \nCounty through Natrona County will see a familiar southwesterly \nbreeze with gusts to 35 mph.\n\nThe trough will approach Tuesday as an open wave. Most guidance \nstill has this digging south into the Great Basin and splitting \nsomewhat as it pulls northward into the Rockies. While this does not \nfavor a particularly noteworthy precipitation event, forecast totals \nhave increased as of late for western Wyoming. Rain and mountain \nsnow will begin late Tuesday morning, spreading eastward through \nTuesday night. Locations east of the Divide can expect another warm \nand windy day ahead of an associated cold front. This front will \nbring snow levels down to the western valleys Tuesday night. \nCurrently total liquid amounts are in the 0.50-0.70\" range across \nthe western mountains and about half as much in the valleys. This \nshould be good enough for 4 to 8 inches in the Tetons, Gros Ventres, \nand western Wind River Mountains, with most of this falling before \nsunrise Wednesday. Western valleys can expect an inch or two of\nsnow accumulation during this period. \n\nMeanwhile, wind will ramp up further Tuesday night east of the \nDivide as a surface low develops across northern Wyoming ahead of \nthe advancing cold front. This will lead to gusts of 50 to 60 mph \nacross the more susceptible areas of the Wind Corridor, though gusts \nof 35 to 45 will be more widespread elsewhere. The best chance for \nprecipitation east of the Divide will be Tuesday night through \nWednesday morning. The Bighorns will be most favored here with 0.25-\n0.50\" of liquid expected; lower elevations will generally see under \na tenth of an inch. Snow levels may drop just low enough for light \nsnow across the basins Wednesday morning, though any accumulation \nis expected to be minimal.\n\nPrecipitation is largely expected to end by Wednesday afternoon. A \ncooler airmass will be in place, with temperatures peaking around \nmore seasonable values. Most models have the next system following \nquickly, with precipitation returning to western Wyoming beginning \nThursday morning. This next system appears to progress similarly to \nthe former, though perhaps a little stronger in magnitude. The \ncurrent forecast reflects this, with more widespread precipitation \nchances for the area from Thursday evening through Saturday morning. \nWith the colder air in place, this could bring light snow to the \nbasins on both sides of the Divide. While uncertainty in the \nprogression and strength of this system remains high, continued \ncooler and unsettled weather is likely through the end of the\nweek.",
